{
  "issue": 17,
  "version": 2,
  "understanding": "The shipped plan's DIAGNOSIS was right and this revision does not rewrite it. The defect it named — the gate never examines an author value, so `authors: [claude]` shipped to readers as `— thanks @claude` — was real, the fix works, and QA confirms it: all eight original acceptance criteria pass, and T-3 through T-20 show `claude`, `github-actions` and any `[bot]` suffix rejected, people accepted, both shipped notes crediting Shivam-Fl, and the downstream changelog render thanking a real person. What the plan got WRONG is that it specified a rule over strings without specifying the TYPE CONTRACT of the values the rule is applied to, and specified no behaviour at all for two boundary inputs. The plan asked for a denylist comparison and said 'lowercase+trim each author'; the implementer wrote `author.toLowerCase()` on every element of a YAML flow sequence. `Array.isArray` guarantees the container, not the elements, and js-yaml types `[12345]` as an int, `[null]` as null, `[true]` as a bool and `[[\"claude\"]]` as a nested list — four shapes a person could plausibly write, all four throwing inside the filter, and nothing between the filter and the top of the script catches it, so the throw escapes `validateFile` as an unhandled rejection. That is BUG-1, and it is a REGRESSION this PR introduces: on base commit 4ebaf1e those same notes exit 0 with 'Validated'. I reproduced all four shapes end to end through the real script at 622c500 and got the TypeError QA reported. The same under-specification produced BUG-2 one level up: the plan listed an empty-list test case without saying what the correct behaviour was, so the implementer supplied a comment asserting that the neighbouring `!data.authors` and `Array.isArray` checks enforce a non-empty list. Neither does — `[]` is truthy, so the first is false, and `Array.isArray([])` is true — and I reproduced that note exiting 0 at 622c500. Both bugs have the shape of the plan rather than the implementer: the happy path's rule was specified to the character and the boundary was left to the reader. A gate reads hostile input, so the boundary is the part that matters. THIS REVISION specifies the input domain, the behaviour at each boundary, and the message shape at each one. It also closes two pre-existing holes sitting directly on this issue's defect class, which the previous attempt left standing: BUG-3, the gate selecting notes with `--diff-filter=A`, which means editing an existing note to credit a bot passes untouched — so this PR's own two corrections, which are edits, are invisible to the very gate it strengthens — and BUG-4, `reportError` interpolating front matter into a workflow-command line with no escaping, where this PR adds a second sink (the author value) to a channel `category` already used. Both are reproduced below and both are cheap. BUG-5, an unquoted `[bot]` dying in js-yaml before the gate's own message is reached, is pre-existing, is a front-matter robustness question with its own design, and is left out of scope and flagged instead. WHAT MY PREVIOUS REVISION GOT WRONG — and the reason it was refused rather than merely reworked — is a single acceptance criterion. AC-12 required that the strings '::add-mask::' and '::notice::' \"never appear as live commands at the start of any output line\", while `reportError` writes `::notice::` and then pipes the release-notes README into stdout on EVERY error path, so a line beginning `::notice::See the [Writing Good Release Notes]…` is emitted whether or not anyone injected anything. That criterion is unsatisfiable by any implementation, including the current one, and it was my error rather than the code's: I wrote the `check` as a blanket prohibition and left the satisfiable rule in the criterion's own `how_to_verify` and in the qa_script line, which both say the narrower and correct thing — no line beginning '::add-mask::', no line beginning '::notice::looks-legit'. I confirmed this by running the fixed gate on the injection payload: the payload collapses into one `::error::` line with `%0A` in place of the newlines, and the only other line is the gate's own README notice. The criterion is therefore corrected to say exactly what its own recipe already said and what the change actually delivers: the ATTACKER-CONTROLLED text cannot begin a line, and the gate's own notice, which carries no front-matter text, is out of scope. That is a criterion brought into line with the rule the plan always meant, not one bent to fit a defect — the defect being fixed is forged workflow commands, and this correction does not excuse a single forged command. Every other criterion, the approach and the file list are carried forward unchanged from the refused revision, and I re-ran all of them against the real script here before re-issuing it.",
  "root_cause": "The plan specified the identity rule but never the type contract of the values the rule is applied to. `findNonPersonAuthors` calls `author.toLowerCase()` on every element of a YAML flow sequence, and the caller's `Array.isArray(data.authors)` guard establishes the container, not the elements: js-yaml types `authors: [12345]` as int, `[null]` as null, `[true]` as bool and `[[\"claude\"]]` as a nested list, each of which throws inside the filter, and with no try/catch between the filter and the top of the script the throw escapes `validateFile` as an unhandled rejection - converting a gate that exited 0 on those notes into one that exits 1 on a stack trace with no `::error::` and no file name. The identical under-specification one level up produced BUG-2: the plan asked for an empty-list test case without stating the intended behaviour, so the implementer wrote a comment asserting that the neighbouring `!data.authors` and `Array.isArray` checks enforce a non-empty list. Neither does (`[]` is truthy; `Array.isArray([])` is true), so the test documents a guarantee the code does not make and the changelog renders '— thanks ' with nobody credited.",
  "approach": "Four changes, all inside packages/ci-actions, none touching a forbidden path. (1) BUG-1, in `findNonPersonAuthors`: make the helper TOTAL over its input. Type-check each element first and treat any non-string - number, null, boolean, nested list, object - as an offender, because none of them is a GitHub username; only then normalise and apply the existing denylist and `[bot]`-suffix rules unchanged. The helper's contract becomes 'return every author that is not a credit to a person', which is one coherent job, keeps the existing seven cases green unchanged (they all pass strings), and makes the call site incapable of throwing no matter what YAML produced. Render non-string offenders with JSON.stringify at the call site so the message names them unambiguously: `[12345].join(', ')` happens to yield \"12345\", but `[null].join(', ')` yields the EMPTY string, which would produce '...not bots or agents: .' and name nothing. (2) BUG-2: tighten the check rather than the comment. Add an explicit `data.authors.length === 0` branch in `validateFile`, with its own message naming the file, so `authors: []` is rejected instead of published as '— thanks '; then correct the empty-list test comment to state what is true after the change. Tightening is the right side of the line here: the hole is reader-visible, and the neighbouring guard plainly intends to close it and fails only because `[]` is truthy; leaving a test that documents a guarantee the code does not make is the worse of the two options QA offered. (3) BUG-3, in the file-selection query: replace the single `--name-only --diff-filter=A` diff with one `--name-status --diff-filter=AM` diff, derive `added` from the rows whose status starts with A so the existing 'No release note added' guard stays byte-identical, and validate the modified rows as well as the added ones. This is strictly additive - no currently-passing branch starts failing for a reason unrelated to its own notes, and the requirement to ADD a note is unchanged. It also closes the loop on this PR's own deliverable: the two notes it corrects are modifications, so until this change the gate could not see them. (4) BUG-4: sanitise at the sink, not at each caller. Add an exported `sanitizeWorkflowCommandData(value)` to util.mjs implementing GitHub's documented escape set for command data - `%`->`%25`, CR->`%0D`, LF->`%0A`, in that order so a value already containing the literal text `%0A` cannot be resurrected into a real newline - and call it on the message inside `reportError`, which covers the author sink this PR added and the category sink that was already there. `:` and `,` are deliberately NOT escaped: they are only special inside the property syntax, and `reportError` emits `::error::${message}` with no properties. Because the check script is a top-level script with side effects and cannot be imported by a test, both new pieces of logic go in util.mjs, the module the script already imports, where they are unit-testable. No network call is introduced anywhere.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.mjs",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "In `findNonPersonAuthors`, type-check each element BEFORE normalising it: if `typeof author !== 'string'` return true (a number, null, boolean, nested list or object is not a GitHub username); only otherwise compute `const normalized = author.toLowerCase().trim()` and apply the existing denylist and BOT_SUFFIX tests unchanged. Update the JSDoc so the contract reads 'returns every author that is not a credit to a person - a known bot or agent, or any value that is not a string - in input order', and note that the type check is what makes the helper safe against arbitrary YAML input rather than only well-formed strings. Also add a second exported function `sanitizeWorkflowCommandData(value)` returning `String(value).replace(/%/g, '%25').replace(/\\r/g, '%0D').replace(/\\n/g, '%0A')`, with a comment saying these are GitHub's documented escapes for workflow-command data, that the order matters because `%` must be escaped first, and that `:` and `,` are left alone because they are only special inside the property syntax, which this script does not emit. Change nothing else in this file - `parseReleaseNotes`, `categoryAutocorrections` and `categoryOrder` are untouched.",
      "why": "The check script is top-level and side-effecting, so it cannot be imported by a test; util.mjs is the module it already imports for its other two validation constants, which makes both new pieces of logic unit-testable and keeps the diff small. The type check lives in the helper rather than at the call site so the exported function cannot throw for any input and the call site stays a single branch."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Four edits. (a) In `reportError`, wrap the message: `console.log(`::error::${sanitizeWorkflowCommandData(message)}`)`, and add `sanitizeWorkflowCommandData` to the existing named import from `../src/release-notes/util.mjs`. This covers every sink at once - the author values this PR added and the category value that was already there. (b) After the existing `Array.isArray(data.authors)` branch, add an empty-list branch: `if (data.authors.length === 0) { reportError(`Release note ${path} has an empty authors list.`); return false; }`, with its own message rather than reusing 'is missing authors', which would be inaccurate - the key is present, the list is not. (c) In the existing non-person-authors branch, render offenders through `JSON.stringify` for any that are not strings, so the message names them unambiguously: `nonPersonAuthors.map(a => (typeof a === 'string' ? a : JSON.stringify(a))).join(', ')`. Plain `join` renders `null` as an empty string, which would emit '...not bots or agents: .' and name nothing. (d) Replace the single `git diff --name-only --diff-filter=A origin/${baseRef}...HEAD` query with one `git diff --name-status --diff-filter=AM` query over the same ref range and path. Parse it into `[status, path]` rows; build `added` from rows whose status starts with `A` and keep the existing `added.length === 0` guard and its message byte-identical; build `changed` from all rows with the same `.md` / README filters as today; and iterate `changed` rather than `added` in the validation loop. Change nothing else in this file - the category checks, the one-line-body check, the existence check and the final success message stay exactly as they are.",
      "why": "This is the only place the gate validates a release note. (a) is the sink both the category field and the author field this PR added reach, so sanitising once covers both and cannot be forgotten by the next caller. (c) exists because the fix for BUG-1 makes non-strings reach the message at all, and a message that silently renders an empty value would be a new defect in place of the old one. (d) is built from `--name-status` rather than a second diff so the added/modified distinction stays available for the 'No release note added' guard, which must keep behaving exactly as it does now."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Keep all seven existing cases and their expectations unchanged - every one passes only strings, so the new type check cannot disturb them. Correct the comment on the 'returns nothing for an empty list' case: delete the sentence 'that is enforced by the missing/array checks in the check script, not here', which is false (`!data.authors` is false for `[]` because arrays are truthy, and `Array.isArray([])` is true), and replace it with a statement of what is now true: the helper has nothing to say about an empty list, and `validateFile` rejects an empty list before calling it. Add the cases listed under tests below. Import `sanitizeWorkflowCommandData` alongside `findNonPersonAuthors` from `./util.mjs`.",
      "why": "The empty-list comment is BUG-2 itself: a test that documents a guarantee the code does not make sends the next maintainer the wrong way, so it is corrected in the same change that makes the guarantee true."
    }
  ],
  "tests": [
    {
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js",
      "action": "edit",
      "cases": [
        "BUG-1, the reproduction: findNonPersonAuthors([12345]) returns [12345] and does not throw - a number is not a GitHub username, so it is an offender rather than a crash",
        "BUG-1, same defect, other shapes: findNonPersonAuthors([null]) returns [null] and findNonPersonAuthors([true]) returns [true] - both throw 'Cannot read properties of null' or 'toLowerCase is not a function' on the code under test",
        "BUG-1, the shape a person would plausibly write: findNonPersonAuthors([['claude']]) returns [['claude']] - a nested list is valid YAML inside a flow sequence and must not crash the gate",
        "BUG-1, mixed and order-preserving: findNonPersonAuthors(['Shivam-Fl', 12345, null, 'claude']) is exactly [12345, null, 'claude'] - people absent, offenders in input order, nothing thrown",
        "helper totality: findNonPersonAuthors never throws for any element js-yaml can produce in a flow sequence - string, int, float, bool, null, list, map",
        "BUG-4: sanitizeWorkflowCommandData of a value containing a newline and '::add-mask::X' returns a single line containing '%0A' and no newline character, so a front-matter value cannot start a new workflow command",
        "BUG-4, the escaping-order trap: sanitizeWorkflowCommandData of a value already containing the literal text '%0A' does not yield a real newline, because '%' is escaped first",
        "BUG-4: sanitizeWorkflowCommandData escapes CR the same way and leaves an ordinary value - a release-note path, or 'claude, github-actions' - byte-identical apart from any '%' it contains"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "acceptance": [
    {
      "id": "AC-1",
      "check": "findNonPersonAuthors returns ['claude'] for the single author 'claude' - the value that shipped in both notes.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "`yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions test` - the case \"returns ['claude'] for authors: ['claude']\" in packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-2",
      "check": "findNonPersonAuthors returns ['github-actions'] for the single author 'github-actions'.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "The case \"returns ['github-actions'] for authors: ['github-actions']\" in the same file."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-3",
      "check": "Any author ending in [bot] is rejected, case-insensitively: 'dependabot[bot]' and 'some-bot[BOT]' both come back, and so does 'claude[bot]'.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "The three [bot] cases in packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js, run by `yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions test`."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-4",
      "check": "A real person's handle passes: findNonPersonAuthors(['Shivam-Fl']) returns [].",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "The case \"returns [] for authors: ['Shivam-Fl']\" in the same file."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-5",
      "check": "A mixed list yields only the offenders, in input order: ['Shivam-Fl', 'claude', 'matt-fidd', 'github-actions'] returns exactly ['claude', 'github-actions'].",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "The mixed-list case in the same file."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-6",
      "check": "End to end: the real gate rejects a note whose only author is 'claude'. Running the check on a scratch branch whose single new commit adds a note with `authors: [claude]` prints `::error::` naming `claude` and exits non-zero, where the same branch printed `Validated 1 release note(s).` and exited 0 before this change.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "BRANCH FROM THE CURRENT HEAD, which carries the fix - `git checkout -b scratch/rn-check` with no start point. Do not branch from origin/main: at QA time this change is on the unmerged PR branch while origin/main is still 4ebaf1e with the unpatched gate, so `git checkout -b scratch/rn-check origin/main` would drop the change under test and run the gate that passes. Confirm first: `git show HEAD:packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.mjs | grep findNonPersonAuthors`. BASE_REF stays `main` - a branch name, never a SHA: the script does `git fetch origin $BASE_REF` then `git diff origin/$BASE_REF...HEAD`, and a raw-SHA fetch writes only FETCH_HEAD, creating no refs/remotes/origin/<sha>, so `origin/<sha>` does not resolve and the script dies before validateFile runs. GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY must be exported on every invocation: reportError pipes README.md into `fs.createWriteStream(process.env.GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY)`, which throws ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE when unset. So: `git checkout -b scratch/rn-check`; add `upcoming-release-notes/tmp-bot-author.md` with front matter `category: Bugfix` / `authors: [claude]` and a single-line body; commit it as the only new commit (pre-commit hook runs nano-staged; use a subject starting with `[AI]`); then `BASE_REF=main GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY=/tmp/rn-summary.md node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs`. Expect a non-zero exit and `::error::Release note upcoming-release-notes/tmp-bot-author.md authors must be GitHub usernames of people, not bots or agents: claude.` Then `git checkout main && git branch -D scratch/rn-check` and remove /tmp/rn-summary.md and the temporary note. (AC-1..AC-5 are the automated coverage; this step proves the wiring from validateFile to the helper, which no unit test reaches.)"
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-7",
      "check": "Both corrected notes now read `authors: [Shivam-Fl]`, and no note left in upcoming-release-notes/ credits a bot or agent: grepping every `authors:` line in the directory finds no `claude`, no `github-actions`, and no value ending in `[bot]`.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "`grep -h '^authors:' upcoming-release-notes/*.md | sort -u` - 39 distinct handles across 83 files, every one a real GitHub handle; the pre-existing ones (MikesGlitch, matt-fidd, Shivam-Fl, ...) are unchanged and the two `claude` entries are gone. `grep -ln '^authors:.*\\(claude\\|github-actions\\|\\[bot\\]\\)' upcoming-release-notes/*.md` returns nothing. Not browser-observable: these files are inputs to a CI gate, not a screen."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-8",
      "check": "The rest of the gate is unharmed: a note with a valid person author, a valid category and a one-line body still passes, and a note with a bad category, a multi-line body or a missing `authors` key still fails as before.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "`yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions test` passes as a whole, and on a scratch branch off the CURRENT HEAD - `git checkout -b scratch/rn-check`, no start point, same reason and same fix as AC-6 - with the same invocation and GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY exported even for the run expected to succeed: with `authors: [Shivam-Fl]` expect `Validated 1 release note(s). 🎉` and exit 0; then `category: Nonsense` expect the category `::error::` and exit 1; then a two-line body expect the one-line-body `::error::` and exit 1; then front matter with no `authors` key at all expect the missing-authors `::error::` and exit 1."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-9",
      "check": "A release note whose authors list contains a value that is not a string is rejected by the gate with an actionable ::error:: naming that value and exit 1, not an uncaught stack trace. This holds for every shape a YAML flow sequence can hold: authors: [12345], authors: [null], authors: [true] and authors: [[\"claude\"]] (a nested list, which is valid YAML and which a person could plausibly write). The error names the value verbatim, so for [12345] it reads 'authors must be GitHub usernames of people, not bots or agents: 12345.' On base commit 4ebaf1e the identical notes all exit 0, so this criterion is the regression fix, not a new restriction.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "Unit: `yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions test` - the four new non-string cases in packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js, which call findNonPersonAuthors with [12345], [null], [true] and [['claude']] and assert each returns the offending value instead of throwing. Gate, end to end: on a scratch branch off the CURRENT HEAD (no start point - origin/main is still the unpatched base at QA time), add four throwaway notes, one per shape, commit them, then run `BASE_REF=main GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY=/tmp/rn-summary.md node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs`. Expect a non-zero exit, a `::error::Release note ...` line naming the file and the value, and NO `TypeError` and NO stack trace anywhere in the output. The gate reports the FIRST failing note and exits, so run each shape on its own branch or amend the note between runs - one error line does not mean three shapes were covered. Confirm the branch carries the fix first with `git show HEAD:packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.mjs | grep findNonPersonAuthors`. See qa_script for the BASE_REF and GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY contracts, which are not optional."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-10",
      "check": "A release note with an empty authors list - authors: [] - is rejected by the gate with an ::error:: and exit 1. It is no longer published as a changelog line ending '— thanks ' with nobody credited, and the empty-list test in util.test.js no longer claims the check script enforces this on its own.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "Gate: on a scratch branch off the CURRENT HEAD, add upcoming-release-notes/qa-empty.md with front matter `category: Bugfix` / `authors: []` and a one-line body, commit it, and run `BASE_REF=main GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY=/tmp/rn-summary.md node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs`. Expect non-zero exit and an `::error::` naming qa-empty.md; the string 'Validated' must NOT appear. Comment: read the 'empty list' case in packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.test.js - its comment must state what is actually true of the code (validateFile rejects an empty list before the helper is called) and must not attribute that to the `!data.authors` or `Array.isArray` checks, which let `[]` through because arrays are truthy."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-11",
      "check": "Editing an existing release note to credit a bot is caught. A branch that adds one perfectly valid note AND edits a pre-existing note to authors: [claude] is rejected, where today the same branch exits 0 because the gate reads only ADDED files. A branch that edits a note and adds none still reports 'No release note added' exactly as it does today - the requirement to add a note is not relaxed by this change.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "This one needs a base that does not contain the note under test, because the gate resolves its base as origin/$BASE_REF and the note has to predate the branch. QA's own recipe: a private bare repo standing in for origin, with the seeded note committed on a base branch pushed there first. Seed upcoming-release-notes/qa-preexisting.md with authors: [Shivam-Fl] on the base commit, then on a branch edit it to authors: [claude] and add upcoming-release-notes/qa-brandnew.md with authors: [Shivam-Fl]; commit both. Run `BASE_REF=<basebranch> GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY=/tmp/rn-summary.md node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs`. Expect non-zero exit and an `::error::` naming qa-preexisting.md and claude. Confirm what was selected with `git diff --name-status --diff-filter=AM origin/<basebranch>...HEAD -- upcoming-release-notes/`, which must list BOTH files - that listing is the change. Then, on a branch that only edits and adds nothing, expect the unchanged 'No release note added' error. Note that once this criterion is satisfied, this PR's own two corrected notes (which are modifications) are finally inspected by the gate; both read `authors: [Shivam-Fl]`, `category: Bugfix` and a one-line body, so both pass."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-12",
      "check": "A front-matter value cannot forge a GitHub Actions workflow command. A note whose sole author is 'innocent\\n::add-mask::GITHUB_TOKEN\\n::notice::looks-legit[bot]' yields exactly one `::error::` line in which both newlines appear as %0A; no output line begins with '::add-mask::'; and no output line begins with '::notice::looks-legit'. The injected text survives only as inert characters inside the ::error:: line. The same holds for the category field, which reached reportError before this change. The gate's own '::notice::' line - the pointer to the release-notes README that reportError writes on every failure - is not part of this claim: it is produced by reportError itself and carries no front-matter text.",
      "verify": "test",
      "how_to_verify": "Unit: `yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions test` - the sanitizeWorkflowCommandData cases in util.test.js, covering LF, CR, and a value already containing the literal text '%0A' (which must not be resurrected into a real newline by the escaping). Gate: on a scratch branch off the CURRENT HEAD add a note whose authors list holds exactly that payload as a double-quoted YAML scalar, commit, run the gate, and read the RAW output rather than the rendered log. Expect exactly one `::error::Release note ...` line containing the payload with %0A in place of the newlines, and NO line beginning '::add-mask::' and NO line beginning '::notice::looks-legit'. A line beginning '::notice::See the [Writing Good Release Notes]' IS expected and is not a failure - reportError writes that itself on every error path. Today that payload emits both injected commands as live lines - reproduced here against commit 622c500, which printed `::error::...not bots or agents: innocent`, then `::add-mask::GITHUB_TOKEN`, then `::notice::looks-legit[bot].` on three separate lines."
    }
  ],
  "qa_script": [
    "There is no browser surface here - this is a CI gate and two markdown files. Verify with the shell; do not open http://localhost:3001 looking for it. AC-1 through AC-12 all carry verify: test for that reason.",
    "Read the diff of packages/ci-actions/src/release-notes/util.mjs: findNonPersonAuthors must type-check before it normalises (the guard is `typeof author !== 'string'`, and it RETURNS TRUE - a non-string is an offender, not a skip), sanitizeWorkflowCommandData must escape '%' before CR and LF, and parseReleaseNotes, categoryAutocorrections and categoryOrder must be untouched. Confirm there is no fetch, no API call and no token anywhere in the change.",
    "Read the diff of packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs: reportError must sanitise the whole message; the empty-list branch must sit after Array.isArray and before the author check; the offender rendering must JSON.stringify non-strings; the diff query must be --name-status --diff-filter=AM with the added-derived guard preserved verbatim. Confirm the category checks, the one-line-body check, the existence check and the success message are unchanged.",
